For each criterion, in the order above, give exactly one assessment:
- "met": the title or abstract shows the criterion is satisfied (for an exclusion criterion: that the reason for exclusion applies);
- "not_met": the title or abstract shows it is not;
- "cannot_tell": the reference says nothing clear about it (missing or vague abstract).
Quote, in "evidence_quote", the exact words of the title or abstract your assessment rests on (in their original language); leave it empty for "cannot_tell" when nothing applies. Never quote words that are not in the reference.

Then give:
- "decision": "include", "exclude" or "uncertain". Exclude only when at least one inclusion criterion is clearly not met or one exclusion criterion clearly applies. If an inclusion criterion cannot be told and no criterion is clearly failed, the decision is "uncertain".
- "inclusion_probability": the probability, from 0 to 1, that a careful reviewer applying these criteria would keep this reference for the full-text stage.
- "rationale": one or two sentences naming the decisive criteria by their codes, written in the language identified by this ISO 639-1 code: {{ language }}.
- "decisive_criteria": the codes of the criteria that decide.

References may be written in any language: apply the criteria to their content.
